grams  must  have at least  0.76 meters cage height and 0.4 meters  floor space  so that means that the minimum cage  dimensions uh  according to the ally guide are six  times smaller than those in the eu  so certainly macaques in the eu are  given  um generally more space  and this is illustrated on this slide  now the advantage of more space of  course means that you can make that  space more complex it's more easily  it's more easy to enrich that space  so the types of enclosures that it's  common to see in  european contract research organizations  or universities  are shown on this next slide  the slides are taking quite a while to  load apologies for that  so gang housing as it's often called is  very much the norm within eu research  establishments and typically um  what you see these days is cages which  are um effectively built into the room  space rather than freestanding um  commercially available uh stainless  steel caging  and this type of caging is really nice  um  it can be very easily enriched  many of the enclosure designs are very  flexible so that you can close down the  space to house smaller groups or expand  it  to house larger groups and we've found  that the animals are less aggressive in  this type of enclosure  they're calmer they're more easily  handled  there are more opportunities for them to  retreat from  animals that they're perhaps not getting  on with they can manage their social  interactions more easily  so there are lots of benefits for  welfare  this type of caging has been approved by  client companies of contract research  organizations  and  regulatory studies have been performed  in this type of housing and  none of the data have been rejected by  the regulators  so here you can see  um  a stainless steel play area on the right  which is adjoined to a bank of stainless  steel caging  in a university  again giving the animals space that can  be enriched and here's a very nice setup  at a uk cro  where you can see group housed animals  with the verandas  with  a  space that can be divided with visual  barriers so the animals can  get away from each other and the animal  at the bottom there coming up through  the pothole that is actually an upper  level to the enclosure that you can see  on the left so that's a  enclosures that run over through floors  and again this means that animals can  get away from each other and manage  their social interactions better  now  this type of housing social housing in  eu compliant cages that are well  enriched is a requirement  under the nc3rs primate guidelines  which we developed in 2006  and which are adopted by the major  funding bodies that you can see on the  bottom there  so if anybody receives funding for  primate research from any of these major  funding bodies then they must provide  social housing and eu size caging and  environmental enrichment wherever that  research is conducted in the world  and this has really led to changes in  practice in many facilities  funded through the uk funders so to just  give you an example  um  working with the facility nc3rs was able  to  encourage  and  insist in fact that  40 al monkeys which were used in a  malaria study in south america were per  housed in cages 3.6 metres cubed instead  of singly housed in cages 10 times  smaller so this is a very powerful  driver for changes in practice  but how have the facilities been able to  adapt what they  normally do in order to meet the  requirements well they've done this in a  number of ways if we look at the first  of all the cage space  in situations where people have  conventional stainless steel type caging  and what they've been able to do is to  remove the dividers between adjoining  cages  enable  in order to give  the animals a larger amount of space  so  within the us for example we would  require that six of these cage units are  joined together um and you can house two  macaques within that space and so  customizing the existing caging is one  way in which you can meet the space  requirements and that has been done in a  number of facilities  an alternative is to  build an additional enclosure we often  call them play areas these can be built  quite cheaply from wood and mesh and  that is a joint to the conventional bank  of caging and we give the animals the  run of the whole space unless you need  to confine them for a veterinary or  scientific purpose and you can enrich  that wood and mesh play area um with  swings ladders perches etc  to really give the animals the  opportunity to perform more natural  behaviors which benefits their welfare  and again this has been done on funded  research projects and can be done  relatively cheaply  i have already mentioned the importance  of a complex environment  for the animals managing their social  interactions um on the left you can see  it's actually a breeding environment for  rhesus macaques um and on the right we  have the san angus macaques and again  you can see the complex space the pop  holes uh the visual barriers which  enable the animals to get away from each  other  and manage their social interactions and  it's really felt anecdotally  that this is  much more successful  housing arrangement for social housing  so moving away from cage space and cage  complexity  it's important to think about  where the animals are sourced from how  you create your groups of animals and  how you monitor them in the long term  so that you have stable groups which  really last  a colleague of mine at the nc3rs  catherine chapman  conducted a survey recently which was  published in 2013  looking at the success of  social housing of macaques used in  toxicology studies  and you can see from that first line  there  that um  eu and non-eu sites are very successful  at socially housing uh adult female  macaques and juvenile macaques but there  is a difference  in the success of social housing of  adult males between those two regions  things that can potentially account for  this  are  whether pairs are established prior to  dosing and whether the pairs are  established utilizing information which  is provided by the supplier about the  compatibility of the animals and their  social history  in the eu sites the animals typically  originate from one group so  compatibility is already partially  established and using behavioral  analysis tools you can identify animals  from within that larger group with which  to form your groups that then go on to  study  and this is something that some cross  have a lot of experience in  and indeed some of the animals will be  supplied with an individual history file  which gives information about  the relationships between the animals um  who they've been grouped with previously  and this means that study directors can  actually use these socialization data to  assign animals to groups um  either  sometimes taking into account the weight  of the animals and sometimes basing the  groups  solely on the socialization data  um  these data uh can be gathered by  observing the animals and looking at  their behavior towards each other  this can be done at the reading center  it can also be done  within the establishment when the  animals arrive  what many people do is to look for  dominance behaviors and subordinates  behaviours performed between individual  animals and by counting these you can  establish the hierarchy within the group  and then select animals for pairs or  groups that show clear dominant  subordinate relationships and in that  way be confident that you have animals  that are likely to maintain that  hierarchy and get on with each other and  actually there's quite a literature  published in this area about pear  formation certainly for adult male and  female macaques  and these data are being used in  practice to create stable groups so you  can look at aggressive behaviors like uh  threatening grimacing yawning and  charging and subordinate behaviors like  lip smacking or rump presentation and  ensure that you are establishing  compatible groups from the start and  then the idea is to maintain those  compatible groups throughout the life  and use of the animal  and it is possible to house animals  socially even during quarantine i know  that some facilities which are set up to  handle very large batches of animals  will house them  singly  but some quarantine facilities are  moving away from that traditional  practice  one issue is the time that is required  to  establish compatible pairs or groups but  if that's done at the  source then  i i wouldn't accept  um that time as a reason why you should  singly house animals  in quarantine  there is concern that if there should be  disease  either individual clinical or  subclinical disease  then this could spread within the room  and this is sometimes proposed as a  reason for singly housing the animals  during quarantine  but to be honest i think common practice  is to treat the entire room  or at least the nearest neighbors  if you do have animals with a clinical  subclinical disease  so if you would do that anyway then it's  not really an argument for single  housing  so  we have successfully challenged the  requirement for single housing in  quarantine  we've also challenged the requirement  for single housing  in  neuroscience studies  using the cax  there is a move i think towards housing  animals with cranial implants  together  it may not be possible at all times but  it is certainly uh the standard within  uk neuroscience facilities  that animals with perennial implants are  housed together  um some of the  newer implant designs  which can be kept  more easily clean and free of infection  do certainly facilitate this but even  animals with  cranial implants  made of dental acrylic  are housed together and  people  do sometimes say oh well you know the  animals can pick at the implants um but  really this is the case whether the  animals are in single housing or group  housing  and we found over the years that there  has not been  um  additional damage to the implant and  you can successfully house neuroscience  animals in this way  similarly it's possible to house  animals on toxicology studies in groups  and this is commonly done within the uk  in the eu  and so even to limited animals  or animals on infusion studies  which will be wearing jackets  uh can be housed socially  um key to this is designing  a good jacket design that the animals  are comfortable with um  and that they  don't try and uh get out of and  very long durations of social housing  can be accommodated um of course the  telemetry devices themselves there are  companies which produce devices which  transmit on multiple frequencies so  you can have a number of telemetered  animals within the group with the device  transmitting on different frequencies or  indeed  where you don't have those devices it's  possible to house a limited animal with  a naive companion which is not too  limited and in that way provide  companionship for those animals  other procedures or other  data that are collected for animals and  toxicology studies  include food and water consumption  and clinical signs  now it is  uh difficult to measure individual food  and water consumption in growth housed  animals um but it is possible to really  question whether you need those  individual food and water consumption  data and whether instead you can observe  the eating and drinking behavior of the  animals and monitor their body weight  and that may well be sufficient to  identify treatment effects that might  reduce appetite  um  similarly  some people say it's more difficult to  record clinical signs such as vomiting  in animals that are group housed  um  that depends on  the monitoring systems that you have in  place for the animals  but  i think it has to be recognized that  actually animals in a socially housed  environment  display more natural behavior  and it may be possible to identify  disturbances to that behavior more  easily for animals that are housed in  groups rather than singly housed animals  which may be  relatively inactive and and quite  depressed within the cage so it's  um more feasible to identify changes in  locomotion uh balance coordination and  certainly interaction with conspecifics  in group housed animals so actually the  group house environment can be more  informative in terms of the effects of  drugs upon the animals  another procedure  which may be performed on toxicology  studies is of course urine collection  um  one should always question whether you  need a total collection of urine or  whether a spot sample would be  sufficient in which case you can train  animals to cooperate for that  if you do need total collection  it may be that you can continue to house  the animals socially but just separate  the individual or individuals  from which you are collecting urine  in a space within the cage and this is  where really flexible enclosure designs  are really important and so here you can  see an example  from a german cro where we have the cage  on the right and at the back of that  cage is a an area where the animal can  be confined  for short periods um for procedures in  this case urine collection and you can  see the setup on the left  which enables you to collect urine from  that animal but keep it within a social  environment  similarly these cage designs which allow  you to  confine animals temporarily  but still allow so  auditory visual olfactory and in some  cases tactile access to their con  specifics can't be used for example  to  measure individual fluid intake for  animals on  fluid control where they're working  for fluid rewards or neuroscience or  behavioral studies you may want to first  of all establish how much fluid that  animal would ordinarily take  when it has free access to fluid so you  know what the baseline fluid intake is  for that animal before you begin your  fluid control regimen and you can do  that by  temporarily dividing the animals within  the enclosure  so that they can't access each other's  water  only their own water bottles you can  measure individual fluid consumption but  still allow them social interactions  even when animals start on the fluid  control regimen it's still possible to  house them in groups  uh for example you could have an animal  on a fluid control regimen  and a naive animal and when the animal  on the fluid control goes off to work  within the laboratory then you can give  the other animal fluid free access to  fluid  it's important to monitor both of those  animals to ensure that they're  achieving sufficient fluid of course and  these areas where you can separate off  the animals but keep them housed  socially are also useful for  post-operative recovery following  surgery  where you want to allow the animal to  space to recover but you don't want to  keep it out of the social group for a  long time in case the hierarchy should  change within that group so that you  when you reintroduce the animal you have  a situation where the animals are  fighting  and the group breaks down so generally  it's thought that it's important to get  the animals back into the social group  as quickly as possible and it may be  that they can recover from surgery in a  safe place but still within that social  environment  another reason that is sometimes given  for single housing of animals is ease of  capture i don't think it's a very good  reason  um  and the comment i wanted to make here is  that  uh establishments that we've worked with  to move away from single housing to  social housing  can often agree to the social housing  without really thinking through  how they're going to cope with that  social housing and perform their studies  at the same time  so for example  if you have large groups of animals and  you need to  capture individuals to perform  sampling procedures or to dose those  animals at given time points you need to  be able to reliably access that animal  for that procedure  and that will involve  training the animals  to  cooperate with capture so that you can  reliably  take your sample and obtain your data  um  this is facilitated by again areas where  you can encourage the animals to come  forward and  sit within an area stationed  in a particular area  that you can  then  capture and restrain that animal and  perform your procedure so for example  some  cros will train animals to come forward  to put their arm through the door of the  cage it's then possible to hold that arm  to open the door of the cage to restrain  the animal as you would do normally and  perform the procedure alternatively you  can train the animals to come to a place  within the procedure like  the small boxes on the front of the  cages on the left  and access the animal in that way  moving on to other types of studies um  it is possible to group house animals  uh in infectious disease studies uh  under containment um here you can see  a state-of-the-art containment system at  a uk laboratory that has been designed  and built for  category 3  infectious agents  in this case  tb studies and the animals  are successfully housed  in groups of up to six  for these studies there is no reason  arising from the barrier system itself  while animals should not be housed in  social groups although  the nature of the experiment  the barrier is designed to contain  may or may not allow  social housing but certainly in these  studies the animals are socially housed  of course it's important that  the animals in each group receive the  same treatment such as the infectious  dose or the vaccination regime  what it is not possible to  house animals uh  that receive live vaccines uh with naive  animals because there could be immune  stimulation of the unvaccinated animals  so in that case um you wouldn't uh house  them  in groups but provided this it received  the same treatment then it is possible  to do that  i've talked a lot about macaques and of  course macaques will be macaques and um  many of you who work and manage with  them will know that  they can be aggressive towards each  other there can be instances of fighting  these are data  published data from a single uk  university  veterinary records for 90 research  macaques used in neuroscience studies  over a 10-year period  and what the data show the graph on the  right is  the number of impacts on the animals  welfare that do not arise from  procedures so it's what we might call  contingent impacts on welfare and you  can see that the majority of the animals  receive one to five of these contingent  impacts on their welfare  over their lifetime  and from the left hand graph the row at  the bottom you can see that the most  frequent  non-procedural impact on welfare is  actually  fight wounds not requiring surgery so we  know that  these  pairs or groups of macaques can  eventually break down and there can be  fighting if the animals are  re-establishing their hierarchy and it  may be  necessary to  separate those animals if they  are continually fighting and causing  damage that requires um  surgery  in that case um generally animals will  be separated  but laboratories would always try to  repair those animals with another  companion  and effectively recreate a new pair or  new group  and if that should break down they may  try a second time  and the long-term aim of course is to  get the animals back into peril group  housing  occasionally animals will be repeatedly  grouping compatible in which case they  may well be singly housed  but it's very important in those cases  to  look to ensure that you do all you can  to  ameliorate the negative effects of  single housing  and many guidelines will recommend that  where single housing is necessary um you  give the animals visual auditory and  olfactory access to companions they may  even be able to interact socially  with a limited period of tactile contact  additional enrichment should be provided  for the welfare of these animals  and if they enjoy human interaction then  positive human interaction  can really contribute to the quality of  life of these animals  that are grouping compatible and  therefore  so  singly housed  so in summary then really the features  that we feel have helped to facilitate  social housing  have been  an increase in enclosure size which  enables you to  then enrich that larger space  make it more complex  in ways that can benefit the animals  ability to perform natural behaviors  which will benefit their welfare and  certainly providing escape areas and  visual barriers and multiple perches  within the enclosure space at different  heights  and multiple for example water bottles  or enrichment devices so that there is  less competition for those resources  within the social group can really help  create a more successful  stable hierarchy  and a social group that lasts in the  long term  flexible enclosures which enable you to  divide the space and temporarily  separate the animals are very important  to facilitate the science while still  allow social housing  um thinking about  how you source and group animals and  obtaining them in pre-existing social  groups and avoiding  breaking up those relationships  can again facilitate long-term social  housing  and identifying and monitoring  compatible animals and observing their  behavior even when they're on study to  ensure that  if there are problems you can intervene  early  always challenge the perceived  scientific or welfare need for single  housing i've given you some examples of  um arguments that have been put forward  for  single housing  in the correspondence that i've had with  applicants for research proposals and  working together we've been able to  really question whether  there is a scientific need  or a welfare need and whether it is  possible to socially house the animals  and indeed in many cases it is and i  think  what has given the facilities the  confidence to move forward with social  housing where they're previously singly  housed  is really the experience of those  establishments that have overcome those  perceived problems  and it's quite powerful for  me for example to say well  all the research groups that i deal with  have  socially housed their animals with  cranial implants for many many years  and  to put the  new laboratory in touch with those  people who have done that for many years  so that they can  learn about the tips and tricks that  enable you to  move forward with social housing so my  last point really is to seek advice from  others about how they have met  the same scientific and regulatory  demands  within a social housing context  and that can give you the information  that you require to  socially house your animals and give  them those animal welfare benefits  and in some cases have the scientific  benefits  and to meet the regulatory requirements  without the need for single housing  so that's the end of my presentation  i hope that you've submitted questions  i'm now going to  take a look  and there aren't any i've probably  stunned you all into  silence it would be great if some of you  were able to  submit some questions  or even some comments you may find that  much of what i've said you do it already  perhaps  or maybe you have some requests for some  additional information about some of the  examples i've given i'd be happy to  answer them if there are any  you
 

